  <h2>1. The Architecture: The Three GPS Segments</h2>
  <p>
    The Global Positioning System is structured into three interdependent segments that work in continuous synchronization:
  </p>

  <div class="step-container">
    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">1</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">The Space Segment (Satellite Constellations)</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          A baseline constellation of 31 operational satellites in Medium Earth Orbit (MEO) at ~20,200 km (12,550 miles) altitude. Distributed across 6 orbital planes inclined at 55° relative to the equator, each satellite circles the globe once every 11 hours and 58 minutes (half a sidereal day), ensuring at least 6 to 10 satellites are visible from virtually any spot on Earth with an open sky view.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">2</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">The Control Segment (Ground Tracking & Correction)</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Maintained by the 2nd Space Operations Squadron (2 SOPS) of the U.S. Space Force at Schriever Space Force Base, Colorado. It includes a Master Control Station, alternate control hubs, 16 monitoring stations worldwide, and ground antennas. Ground stations constantly track satellite orbits, measure atomic clock drift, and upload updated orbital parameters (ephemeris) and system health data (almanac).
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <div class="step-number">3</div>
      <div class="step-body">
        <h4 class="step-title">The User Segment (Your Phone & Receivers)</h4>
        <p class="step-desc">
          Every smartphone, vehicle navigation system, smartwatch, and marine receiver. GPS receivers are <em>completely passive</em>: they only receive radio signals and never transmit data back to satellites. Your privacy is preserved at the orbital level because satellites have zero knowledge of who is receiving their broadcast signals.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>2. The Core Mathematical Principle: 3D Trilateration</h2>
  <p>
    GPS determines position through <strong>spherical trilateration</strong>. While triangulation relies on measuring angular vectors, trilateration relies strictly on measuring <em>distances</em> from known reference points.
  </p>

  <h3>How Distance Is Computed: Time-of-Flight (ToF)</h3>
  <p>
    GPS satellites continuously transmit pseudo-random noise (PRN) radio codes encoded with the precise timestamp at which the transmission left the satellite antenna. When your receiver detects this radio signal, it matches the PRN sequence against an internally generated replica to compute the transit time delay (Δt):
  </p>

  <blockquote>
    <strong>Pseudorange (P)</strong> = Speed of Light (c) × Transit Time Delay (Δt)<br />
    where c ≈ 299,792,458 meters/second
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    Because radio waves travel at the speed of light, light covers approximately <strong>30 centimeters (1 foot) per nanosecond (10⁻⁹ s)</strong>. Knowing the exact distance from one satellite means your receiver is situated somewhere on the 3D spherical shell centered on that satellite.
  </p>

  <h3>Why Four Satellites Are Required (The 4th Clock Dimension)</h3>
  <p>
    In pure Euclidean 3D geometry, intersecting three spheres yields exactly two intersection points (one located on Earth's surface and the other deep in outer space). However, real-world GPS calculations require solving for <strong>four unknowns (X, Y, Z, Δt_r)</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Spatial Coordinate X:</strong> ECEF Cartesian X-axis</li>
    <li><strong>Spatial Coordinate Y:</strong> ECEF Cartesian Y-axis</li>
    <li><strong>Spatial Coordinate Z:</strong> ECEF Cartesian Z-axis</li>
    <li><strong>Receiver Clock Bias (Δt_r):</strong> Timing offset between your phone's crystal oscillator and satellite atomic clocks</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    While satellites carry ultra-stable rubidium or cesium atomic clocks costing hundreds of thousands of dollars, consumer smartphones rely on quartz crystal oscillators. An error of just 1 microsecond (0.000001 s) in your phone's clock would cause a positional error of <strong>300 meters</strong>! The fourth satellite provides the fourth algebraic equation needed to mathematically cancel out receiver clock drift.
  </p>

