  <h2>3. Einstein's Relativity in Daily GPS Operations</h2>
  <p>
    GPS is one of the very few consumer technologies that must account for both of Albert Einstein's theories of relativity to prevent total operational failure:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Special Relativity (Time Dilation):</strong> Because GPS satellites move at orbital velocities of ~14,000 km/h relative to ground observers, their clocks tick <em>slower</em> by approximately <strong>7 microseconds per day</strong>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>General Relativity (Gravitational Time Dilation):</strong> Because satellites orbit in a significantly weaker gravitational field at 20,200 km altitude, spacetime is less curved, causing satellite clocks to tick <em>faster</em> by approximately <strong>45 microseconds per day</strong>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Combining both effects (+45 - 7 = +38 microseconds/day), satellite atomic clocks run faster than Earth-bound clocks by <strong>38 microseconds every day</strong>. Without programmatic relativistic clock offsets calibrated into satellite synthesizers, GPS navigation calculations would accumulate a positional drift of <strong>11 kilometers (6.8 miles) per day</strong>!
  </p>

  <h2>4. The WGS 84 Geodetic Datum & Coordinate Projection</h2>
  <p>
    Once your receiver calculates its 3D Cartesian coordinates $(X, Y, Z)$ in the Earth-Centered, Earth-Fixed (ECEF) reference frame, it projects those values onto a standardized mathematical reference ellipsoid: <strong>WGS 84 (World Geodetic System 1984)</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    WGS 84 models Earth as an oblate spheroid with:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Semi-major axis (Equatorial radius $a$):</strong> 6,378,137.0 meters</li>
    <li><strong>Flattening ($f$):</strong> 1 / 298.257223563</li>
    <li><strong>Semi-minor axis (Polar radius $b$):</strong> ~6,356,752.314 meters</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    From this ellipsoidal model, the receiver derives your human-readable <a href="/tools/latitude-longitude/">Latitude and Longitude</a> and ellipsoidal height. To learn more about coordinate conversions between geodetic datums, consult our guide on <a href="/guides/utm-coordinates-and-datums/">UTM Coordinates & Datums</a> or use our <a href="/tools/coordinate-converter/">Coordinate Converter</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Multi-GNSS Constellations: Beyond Standard GPS</h2>
