  <h2>6. Dual-Frequency (L1 + L5) Revolution</h2>
  <p>
    For decades, consumer electronics only received the legacy <strong>L1 frequency (1575.42 MHz)</strong>. Modern flagship devices (iPhone 14/15/16 Pro, Google Pixel 7/8/9, Galaxy S23/S24) feature <strong>dual-frequency GNSS</strong> receiving both L1 and the newer <strong>L5 frequency (1176.45 MHz)</strong>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Ionospheric Error Cancellation:</strong> The ionosphere slows down radio waves proportional to the inverse square of signal frequency ($1/f^2$). Comparing arrival times across L1 and L5 allows receivers to calculate and subtract ionospheric delay entirely.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Multipath Rejection in Urban Canyons:</strong> L5 has a 10x higher chipping rate (10.23 MHz vs 1.023 MHz), making reflected signals bouncing off skyscrapers easily distinguishable from direct line-of-sight signals.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you are experiencing inaccurate positional fixes in dense cities or indoors, explore our comprehensive troubleshooting walkthrough: <a href="/guides/improve-gps-accuracy/">How to Improve GPS Accuracy</a>.
  </p>

